# {{ _copier_conf.dst_path.name }}

A [Mistral Search Toolkit](https://pypi.org/project/mistralai-search-toolkit/) project with a Postgres (pgvector) backend.

SDK namespace: `mistralai.search.toolkit` — see the [toolkit docs](https://github.com/mistralai/mistral-pro/tree/main/dashboards/main/search/toolkit) for architecture and extension guides.

## Setup

```bash
make installdeps
```

`MISTRAL_API_KEY` is set in `.env` from the value you entered during `copier copy` (or edit `.env` later). Project name matches the folder you passed to `copier copy` (this directory).

The Postgres connection string lives in `.env` as `POSTGRES_DSN` (defaults to the
local dev container: `postgresql://pg:1234@localhost:5432/postgres`).

## Commands

### Start Postgres and migrate the schema

```bash
make setup-postgres
```

This starts the `pgvector/pgvector:pg18` container (port **5432**) and then runs
`make migrate` (`alembic upgrade head`), which applies the chain in `alembic/versions/`.
Safe to rerun: Alembic records what it has already applied.

To wipe local Postgres data and start fresh:

```bash
make reset-postgres
make setup-postgres
```

### Changing the schema

The collection is stated three times, on purpose, each pinned against the others:

| Where | What it is | Checked by |
|---|---|---|
| `POSTGRES_COLLECTION` in `src/search_app/__init__.py` | the declaration this app binds to | — |
| `alembic/versions/` | the chain that builds it, as static DDL | `alembic check` (`tests/test_migrations.py`) |
| `alembic/structure.sql` | a checked-in render of the declaration, so schema changes show up in review | `tests/test_structure.py` |

So to add a field or retune the index:

```bash
# 1. edit the declaration in src/search_app/__init__.py
make structure                                        # 2. re-render the snapshot
uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add field" # 3. write the matching revision
make migrate                                          # 4. apply it
make test                                             # all three now agree
```

Revisions are deliberately static DDL rather than a call back into the toolkit: anything that
resolved the table's shape at migration time would let a toolkit upgrade change what an old
revision does. The one exception is `op.build_vector_index`, which takes exactly what the
generated `op.create_index` takes and builds it `CONCURRENTLY` instead — a rollout choice
autogenerate cannot express.

### Changing the schema while the previous release is still serving

The store names its columns explicitly in every statement — it never does `SELECT *` — so it
ignores database columns it does not declare, and fails on declared columns the database lacks.
Everything else follows from that one rule: **the database must be a superset of every
declaration currently running.**

| Change | Releases | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Add a field | 1 | Old pods never name the column; keep it nullable or server-defaulted and they write NULL. Migrate before deploying. |
| Remove a field | 2 | The column must outlive the release that stopped writing it. Release N drops the field from the model and adds `"<table>.<column>"` to `PENDING_DROP`; release N+1 drops the column and the entry. |
| Retune HNSW (`m`, `ef_construction`) | 1 | Transparent. Postgres picks an index by structure, not name, so the rebuilt index answers the old pods' queries unchanged — build it with `op.build_vector_index` and drop the old one in the same revision. |
| Change the distance metric | 2 | Changes the operator class, so old pods' queries cannot use the new index and fall back to a sequential scan. Release N builds the new index and leaves the old one, adding its name to `PENDING_DROP`; release N+1 drops the index and the entry. |
| Change the embedding dimension | — | Not migratable in place. Old pods would write vectors of the wrong width into the new column type; this needs a second collection and a cutover. |

`PENDING_DROP` lives in `src/search_app/__init__.py` and covers both halves — columns as
`"<table>.<column>"`, indexes by name. It is deliberately narrow: only objects the database has
and the declaration does not, and only the ones listed, so a retirement window is visible in
review and cannot quietly become permanent.

